<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>What Is a One-Arm Dead Hang</h2>
      <p>A one-arm dead hang suspends your full bodyweight from a single hand on the bar. Your opposite arm hangs at your side or rests across your chest. Every kilogram you weigh passes through five fingers and one wrist.</p>
      <p>The load per hand doubles compared to a standard two-arm hang. A 180 lb person puts about 90 lbs through each hand during a two-arm hang and 180 lbs through one hand during a one-arm hang. This makes it one of the most demanding bodyweight grip exercises.</p>
      <p>One-arm dead hangs test maximum grip strength rather than grip endurance. Hold times drop from minutes to seconds. The exercise exposes weaknesses in finger strength and shoulder stability that two-arm hangs can hide.</p>

      <h2>Benefits</h2>
      <p>Some people use one-arm hangs as supplementary grip work for deadlifts, climbing and grappling. Each hand carries the whole load. The flexor tendons, finger pulleys and forearm muscles work harder than in a two-arm hang.</p>
      <p>One-arm hangs show the difference between your hands. Most people grip a little harder with one hand; about 10% is the figure usually quoted for right-handers, with little difference in left-handers.</p>
      <p>Climbers often load one hand at a time while the other reaches for the next hold. One-arm hangs are one way to train that.</p>
      <p>Some martial artists also use one-arm hangs as grip work for holding fabric and controlling a wrist.</p>

      <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
      <p>Start one-arm progressions only after a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold. Skipping the two-arm base raises the chance of finger and elbow irritation.</p>
      <p>Complete about 30 seconds of <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">active hang</a> with both arms. Shoulder blade control matters more in one-arm work because the entire load passes through one shoulder joint. Weak shoulder stabilizers make one-arm hangs harder to control and may irritate the shoulder.</p>
      <p>Train pain-free for 4+ weeks at bodyweight. Any nagging shoulder, elbow or wrist pain is a reason to wait on one-arm attempts. See a clinician about it first.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Ready Checklist</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>About 60-second two-arm dead hang</li>
          <li>About 30-second two-arm active hang</li>
          <li>4+ weeks pain-free hanging</li>
          <li>No active shoulder, elbow or wrist injuries</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Progression to One-Arm</h2>
      <p>The path from two-arm to one-arm uses a systematic finger-reduction method. Each phase removes assistance from your off-hand until you hang from one arm alone. The pace depends on bodyweight, grip and shoulder strength. Some people do not reach a full one-arm hang.</p>

      <h3>Phase 1: Two-Arm Base</h3>
      <p>Build your two-arm dead hang to about 60 seconds. This base gives your working arm enough endurance to absorb the increased demand as assistance decreases.</p>

      <h3>Phase 2: Four-Finger Assist</h3>
      <p>Grip the bar fully with your working hand. Place only four fingers of your off-hand on the bar beside it. Keep your off-hand grip light. Use it for balance and minimal support only. Target 15-20 seconds per set.</p>

      <h3>Phase 3: Three-Finger Assist</h3>
      <p>Remove one finger from your assist hand. Three fingers on the bar provide less support and shift more load to the working arm. Hold for 12-15 seconds per set. Spend 1-2 weeks at this phase before progressing.</p>

      <h3>Phase 4: Two-Finger Assist</h3>
      <p>Two fingers on the bar provide minimal stability. Your working arm now carries most of your bodyweight. Hold for 10 to 12 seconds per set. This phase reveals whether your grip and shoulder are ready for full one-arm work.</p>

      <h3>Phase 5: One-Finger Assist</h3>
      <p>A single finger on the bar acts as a safety net rather than real support. Your working arm handles nearly all of the load. Hold for 8 to 10 seconds. Many people spend about 1 week at this phase before removing the assist entirely.</p>

      <h3>Phase 6: Full One-Arm Hang</h3>
      <p>Remove your assist hand completely. Hold for 5 seconds on your first attempt and then build toward 10 to 15 seconds.</p>

      <h2>Proper One-Arm Form</h2>
      <p>Center your grip directly above your shoulder on the bar. Off-center placement creates lateral torque that your core must fight constantly. Centering the hand reduces rotational demand and extends hold time.</p>
      <p>Slight body rotation toward the hanging arm is normal and expected. Your body will turn slightly toward the hanging arm. Fighting this rotation wastes energy. Allow it.</p>
      <p>Keep your opposite arm relaxed at your side or resting across your abdomen. Flailing the free arm creates momentum that destabilizes your grip. Stillness preserves grip endurance.</p>
      <p>Breathe steadily through your nose. Short shallow breaths often mean the hold is nearly over. Deep controlled breaths suggest you have time left.</p>

      <h2>Programming</h2>
      <p>Train one-arm hangs on non-consecutive days with at least one rest day between hard sessions. Daily one-arm work raises the risk of flexor tendon and finger pulley irritation.</p>
      <p>Perform 3-4 sets per arm each session. Start with your weaker arm while your grip is freshest. Alternate arms with 90 to 120 seconds of rest between sets. Match the volume on both sides.</p>
      <p>Target 5-15 seconds per set depending on your current level. Short holds of 5-8 seconds build peak strength. Longer holds of 10-15 seconds build strength endurance. Vary the target across the training week. These doses are an example. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Weekly Schedule Example</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Monday:</strong> 4 sets/arm x 8-10s. Strength focus.</li>
          <li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> 3 sets/arm x 12-15s. Endurance focus.</li>
          <li><strong>Friday:</strong> 3 sets/arm x best controlled hold. Test session.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>Track hold times for each arm separately. Many people aim to bring the weaker side close to the stronger side. The <a href="/one-arm-hang-guide/">step-by-step one-arm hang guide</a> lays the build-up out week by week.</p>

      <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
      <h3>Skipping the Progression</h3>
      <p>Jumping straight to one-arm hangs without the finger-assist phases overloads tendons that have not adapted to unilateral force. The A2 and A4 finger pulleys are especially vulnerable. A finger that hurts at its base needs a clinician's assessment. Follow every phase even if you feel strong enough to skip ahead.</p>

      <h3>Ignoring the Weaker Arm</h3>
      <p>Training only your dominant arm feels productive but widens the gap between hands. Train your weaker arm first in every session and give it equal volume.</p>

      <h3>Forgetting to Warm Up</h3>
      <p>A warm-up helps you feel ready; evidence that it prevents injury is limited. Spend 2 to 3 minutes on the <a href="/mobility/">mobility warm-up</a> before any one-arm work: arm circles, wrist circles, band pull-aparts and one or two short, easy hangs.</p>

      <h3>Gripping Too Hard</h3>
      <p>Over-squeezing the bar wastes energy. Grip firmly but do not crush the bar. Your fingers need only enough force to prevent the bar from sliding through your hand.</p>

      <h2>One-Arm Hang Time Targets</h2>
      <p>No published standard exists for one-arm hang time. Track your own best hold per arm. Bar diameter, chalk, hand size, bodyweight and finger proportions all change a one-arm hold. Compare each arm with its own earlier holds on the same bar.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>How do I progress to a one-arm dead hang?</h3>
        <p>To progress to a one-arm dead hang start with a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold. Then move to finger-assisted hangs where your off-hand uses 4 fingers for minimal support. Reduce the assist from 4 fingers to 3 to 2 to 1 over about 1 to 2 weeks per phase. Then remove the assist hand entirely.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Is a one-arm dead hang bad for your shoulder?</h3>
        <p>A one-arm dead hang is a high-load exercise for the shoulder. Each arm carries about twice the load it carries in a two-arm hang. Build up through the steps above and complete about 30 seconds of active hang with both arms first. Stop if you feel sharp shoulder pain. See a clinician before trying it with a shoulder condition.</p>
      </div>

